---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Contact | overtimechecker.com';
const description = 'Contact overtimechecker.com for questions, corrections, or feedback on our overtime pay calculator and worker-rights guides.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Contact</h1>
  <p>Email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We're a small team, so email is the only channel we monitor &mdash; there's no live chat or phone line, and that's by design: overtime questions deserve a considered written answer, not a rushed one.</p>

  <h2>What we respond to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Corrections to overtime math or citations.</strong> FLSA regulations, salary thresholds, and state overtime statutes all get updated periodically. If a page cites a formula, threshold, or statute that's out of date, tell us which page and what the correct figure is; we verify against the primary source before making any change.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors or unclear language in guides.</strong> If a sentence in a guide is ambiguous, contradicts itself, or is just hard to follow, we want to know &mdash; plain language is one of our core editorial standards.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggestions for new content.</strong> If you searched for an overtime question and couldn't find it on the Site &mdash; a state we don't cover in enough depth, a pay structure the calculator doesn't handle &mdash; let us know what you were trying to figure out.</li>
    <li><strong>Press and partnership inquiries.</strong> Journalists covering wage theft or labor policy, and businesses interested in advertising, can reach the same address.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to expect</h2>
  <p>We read every message. Corrections and editorial questions typically get a reply within a few business days. If a correction is verified, we update the affected page and note the date of the material change, consistent with our <a href="/editorial/">corrections policy</a>. We don't always reply to content suggestions individually, but we do track them for future guides.</p>

  <h2>What we don't respond to</h2>
  <p>We can't evaluate your specific pay dispute, review your actual paystubs or timesheets, or tell you exactly how much you're owed &mdash; that requires someone who can see your full pay records and your employer's exemption classification for your role. We also don't recommend specific attorneys, and we don't accept payment to feature or promote anyone on the Site. If you email us asking for case-specific advice, we'll point you back to this policy and to the guidance below.</p>

  <h2>Before you email</h2>
  <p>Many questions we get are already answered on the Site: check the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> for common questions on exempt status, comp time, and off-the-clock work, or the <a href="/guides/">guides</a> &mdash; including <a href="/guides/how-overtime-is-calculated/">how overtime is calculated</a>, <a href="/guides/state-overtime-laws-explained/">state overtime laws explained</a>, <a href="/guides/what-to-do-if-your-employer-wont-pay-overtime/">what to do if your employer won't pay overtime</a>, and <a href="/guides/common-overtime-misconceptions/">common overtime misconceptions</a>. The <a href="/state-laws/">state laws page</a> has the full state-by-state breakdown, and <a href="/know-your-rights/">Know Your Rights</a> covers exemption and retaliation basics. If your question is about how the calculator arrived at a number, the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> walks through the federal and state formulas in detail, with a worked example.</p>

  <h2>If you think you're owed real money</h2>
  <p>We don't make referrals, but a couple of things are worth doing before you talk to anyone. First, start gathering your own records now: pay stubs, timesheets or clock-in data, your written job description, and any texts or emails about hours or bonuses &mdash; employer records aren't always complete or accurate, and yours may be the better evidence. Second, know your deadline: the federal FLSA gives you two years to file a claim (three if the violation was willful), and some states allow longer, so don't sit on this. For a single pay period's shortfall, filing a complaint with the U.S. Department of Labor's Wage and Hour Division or your state labor agency is free and doesn't require a lawyer &mdash; they can investigate and order back pay. If the underpayment has gone on for a while, involves misclassification (being called "exempt" when your duties say otherwise), or looks like it's happening to coworkers too, it's worth a consultation with a wage-and-hour employment attorney: these cases often qualify as collective or class actions under the FLSA, and most attorneys in this practice area work on contingency, so there's usually no upfront cost to find out where you stand.</p>
</BaseLayout>
